From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Manuel Pereira <manuelpereiralds@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 19:20:47 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] PC5: thread and fiber stacks commit what Windows commits
 (Wine patch 0604)

prospero-win d393828, after the v0.2.0 PC5 builds on, picked onto PC5's
patches.

Wine committed the whole stack of every thread and fiber, and on the
console every committed page is direct memory at once. Assetto Corsa EVO
creates about 2,750 fibers at startup with its exe's 1 MiB reserve and
4 KiB commit, which cost about 2.7 GiB of direct memory (#716).

Patch 0604 commits what Windows commits: the commit size at the top of
the stack, rounded up to 16 KiB host pages, with a guard host page under
it, growing through the guard page as Wine's stacks always could. The
Unix side lays out thread stacks this way; RtlCreateUserStack does it for
fibers in a title (WINE_PS5_NTDLL_DIR). Wine itself writes past a guard
page from below: signal_start_thread clears the 60 KiB under a new
thread's initial context, and a user callback's arguments can be larger
than a guard page (a whole WM_GETTEXT buffer). So a fault on a reserved
page of the thread's own stack, under its guard page, commits up to the
stack limit; without that, wineboot died at its first thread start. A
fiber now starts with 16 KiB committed, a thread with 64 KiB. With 4 KiB
host pages, on a PC, stacks are committed whole as before.

tests/test_wine_fiber_stacks.py runs the PC's Wine with
WINE_HOST_PAGE_SIZE=16384: 3000 fibers commit 16 KiB each against
1,040,384 bytes before (49 MB against 3.1 GB), a fiber and a thread
recurse 600 KiB through their guard pages and take a 200 KB WM_GETTEXT
buffer, and an overflow raises STATUS_STACK_OVERFLOW on both, in 64-bit
and WoW64 processes. Before the patch a fiber's overflow under 16 KiB
host pages ended the process ("stack overflow ... unrecoverable"): its
4 KiB guard page shared a host page with the no-access page.

On the console (2026-10-28), the same program run as a game: 3000 fibers
committed 49 MB, against 3.12 GB and then a crash before the patch; 1000
fibers in a WoW64 process on the native 32-bit backend, 16 MB; growth and
overflow passed on both. Half-Life 2's menu, OpenArena with bots, the
Direct3D 12 control and Minesweeper ran as before (frame rate and CPU
within run-to-run noise).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
